        cor:rationale "HOLD — recorded, not accepted. cor:expectedVerdict stays cor:Unadjudicated and NO cor:scoreFloor is pinned, deliberately: see the last sentence. Escalation-reason census 2026-08-20 (HEAD 820ab24), § Seam 1: score 0.3438, 7 pages, 10 escalation records. § Token accounting for the same run reads 229 asserted / 437 escalated tokens — bfs escalates almost twice what it asserts. Census § Where the escalations actually are: ROUND_TRIP_FAIL x5, ALL on p5 and ALL region-level #htable{n}-rt URIs (holon.py:493); KIND_NOT_SUPPORTED x3 (p0 the press-release masthead, p6 x2); REGION_TILING_FAILED x2 (p4 and p5, chart captions with mangled glyph runs). Read as DEFECTS rather than as firings the surface is small and legible: one round-trip mechanism failing on one page, one region kind the reader declines to support, one glyph-run problem confined to chart captions. Two cautions this note records so a later reader does not overread the counts. First, the census warns that ROUND_TRIP_FAIL is two mechanisms under one label — region-level (holon.py:493) and cell-level (holon.py:55) — and all 5 firings here are region-level, so this 5 is no evidence at all about the cell-level emitter, which the corpus never exercises. Second, and this is the reason the hold is recorded rather than the floor pinned: cor:scoreFloor is a regression guard, so nothing in the vocabulary stops a floor being pinned at 0.3438 and this document being called met without one glyph of it being read correctly. That is refused here explicitly, on the standing rule that a bar is never lowered to meet it. Held." ] .

